1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:17,540
Well, welcome to DDD Europe, and thank you for the welcome.

2
00:00:18,540 --> 00:00:27,840
So today, my talk, I think the one I'm assigned is sort of what is domain-driven design.

3
00:00:27,840 --> 00:00:35,320
And so I'll try to get across sort of the basic concepts and the motivations,

4
00:00:36,000 --> 00:00:40,880
and then they'll go into more detail in some of the other talks about some of the important principles,

5
00:00:40,940 --> 00:00:42,480
as you've seen on the agenda.

6
00:00:43,560 --> 00:00:48,100
I always like to start the discussion with this statement.

7
00:00:48,280 --> 00:00:51,260
It's like a mantra, almost, that I do.

8
00:00:51,260 --> 00:00:57,480
Because a lot of people come to design with deep-seated perfectionist tendencies.

9
00:00:58,340 --> 00:01:02,720
And those tendencies undermine all our attempts to do good design.

10
00:01:03,060 --> 00:01:06,640
And this I say as someone who has this tendency.

11
00:01:07,220 --> 00:01:12,060
And speaking to a room full of, I'm sure, 90% of you have this tendency, too.

12
00:01:12,720 --> 00:01:16,300
So keep that in the back of your mind as we go along.

13
00:01:17,240 --> 00:01:20,400
Now, there are a lot of people who don't have that tendency.

14
00:01:20,960 --> 00:01:26,660
And when they look at some of the things we do, and they'll say, you know, you don't need all that.

15
00:01:27,840 --> 00:01:28,740
Truth and beauty.

16
00:01:29,120 --> 00:01:30,320
It's just truth and beauty.

17
00:01:30,720 --> 00:01:32,840
And all you need is software that runs.

18
00:01:33,900 --> 00:01:36,960
And what we're doing is about software, right?

19
00:01:37,040 --> 00:01:42,940
We're really, our goal is to create software that does an important job.

20
00:01:43,720 --> 00:01:49,940
And so what is the most effective way of creating software that does an important job?

21
00:01:50,380 --> 00:01:57,480
It isn't a given that that would be models or a particular style of doing models.

22
00:01:57,840 --> 00:02:00,340
That shouldn't be taken for granted.

23
00:02:00,780 --> 00:02:02,940
We should always be questioning our assumptions.

24
00:02:03,760 --> 00:02:06,760
But I think there is a good case for it.

25
00:02:07,140 --> 00:02:11,640
And I will try to illustrate that case with an example.

26
00:02:12,620 --> 00:02:14,200
All this stuff is so abstract.

27
00:02:15,480 --> 00:02:17,740
I like to get straight to an example.

28
00:02:18,840 --> 00:02:22,160
So mostly in this talk, I'll go through this.

29
00:02:23,260 --> 00:02:26,660
The example is from a domain of container shipping.

30
00:02:26,660 --> 00:02:27,780
This is for bulk.

31
00:02:27,840 --> 00:02:29,140
Cargo transport.

32
00:02:30,020 --> 00:02:34,540
And most people, I think, are at least a little bit familiar with this.

33
00:02:36,180 --> 00:02:41,960
Almost all bulk cargo these days is loaded into containers like this one.

34
00:02:42,600 --> 00:02:46,500
And then put onto ships like this to go across the ocean.

35
00:02:47,240 --> 00:02:51,940
Transfer it in ports like this to go onto other ships or onto trains.

36
00:02:52,180 --> 00:02:55,940
You just simply attach a chassis to the bottom of the container.

37
00:02:56,740 --> 00:02:57,820
And it becomes a train.

38
00:02:58,000 --> 00:02:59,100
It becomes a train car.

39
00:02:59,100 --> 00:03:00,920
Or a different kind of chassis.

40
00:03:01,080 --> 00:03:04,120
And it becomes the trailer of a truck.

41
00:03:04,660 --> 00:03:10,460
And so this kind of intermodal transportation, it's very efficient.

42
00:03:10,880 --> 00:03:13,860
And you can imagine, it has some logistical complexity.

43
00:03:15,080 --> 00:03:18,920
And because of that logistical complexity, there's a lot of software involved.

44
00:03:20,000 --> 00:03:21,920
So I think that...

45
00:03:24,120 --> 00:03:25,600
Well, anyway.

46
00:03:26,940 --> 00:03:27,080
So...

47
00:03:27,820 --> 00:03:34,700
Imagine that we work in a company, a shipping company.

48
00:03:34,700 --> 00:03:39,340
And our job is to write software that addresses those logistical issues.

49
00:03:40,520 --> 00:03:48,120
So a typical kind of problem, a kind of case that we might be looking at,

50
00:03:48,220 --> 00:03:53,380
would involve, say, a customer calling one of our clerks and saying,

51
00:03:53,700 --> 00:03:56,160
I have a container in Hong Kong.

52
00:03:56,280 --> 00:03:57,620
I want to ship it to Dallas.

53
00:03:57,620 --> 00:03:57,700
Taxi.

54
00:03:57,700 --> 00:03:57,800
Taxi.

55
00:03:57,800 --> 00:04:00,700
So the clerk might say, all right.

56
00:04:01,040 --> 00:04:04,320
They put into the computer, the origin is Hong Kong.

57
00:04:04,820 --> 00:04:06,240
The destination is Dallas.

58
00:04:06,920 --> 00:04:10,640
And the computer will come up with a routing for that cargo.

59
00:04:11,660 --> 00:04:17,260
And then they will explain it back to the customer.

60
00:04:17,340 --> 00:04:17,680
All right.

61
00:04:17,740 --> 00:04:22,900
Your cargo would be loaded in Hong Kong onto a specific vessel voyage.

62
00:04:23,080 --> 00:04:25,740
ABC is the designator of that in this picture.

63
00:04:26,680 --> 00:04:27,680
And then in Hong Kong...

64
00:04:27,680 --> 00:04:30,760
In Hong Kong, it would be transferred onto a train, XYZ.

65
00:04:31,040 --> 00:04:39,960
And that would get it to Texas, to Dallas, Texas, on a certain date and time.

66
00:04:40,460 --> 00:04:42,500
The customer says, all right, that sounds good.

67
00:04:42,640 --> 00:04:43,020
Book it.

68
00:04:44,200 --> 00:04:48,600
So then they take a few more steps, and it's stored in the computer.

69
00:04:48,900 --> 00:04:50,440
This is our first use case.

70
00:04:51,620 --> 00:04:56,880
And we could start immediately thinking about, all right, what kind of objects do we need?

71
00:04:56,880 --> 00:04:57,660
Or what kind of functions?

72
00:04:57,660 --> 00:05:05,100
And we could develop a design and a model for this thing.

73
00:05:05,800 --> 00:05:09,740
But that ignores one of the realities of software development.

74
00:05:10,600 --> 00:05:14,280
And that is that we already have software that does this.

75
00:05:14,940 --> 00:05:22,780
In almost every project, we're working in a problem space where there's already a lot of software.

76
00:05:23,220 --> 00:05:25,940
Our software will have to coexist with that software.

77
00:05:26,140 --> 00:05:27,540
Perhaps it will be...

78
00:05:27,660 --> 00:05:31,060
Perhaps we're talking about a refinement of the existing software.

79
00:05:31,580 --> 00:05:37,320
Perhaps we're talking about some new piece of software that will integrate with another piece of software.

80
00:05:37,720 --> 00:05:42,620
Or maybe we're talking about a new piece of software that's replacing a piece of existing software.

81
00:05:42,840 --> 00:05:45,220
But there's always existing software.

82
00:05:45,740 --> 00:05:49,860
Even in so-called greenfield projects, there's still existing software.

83
00:05:50,520 --> 00:05:52,620
And that's a very important constraint.

84
00:05:53,620 --> 00:05:56,380
So I don't like to ignore that constraint.

85
00:05:57,660 --> 00:06:04,780
And a number of the techniques that will be talked about later,

86
00:06:04,960 --> 00:06:08,480
some of those are related to that, especially context mapping.

87
00:06:09,380 --> 00:06:15,340
But for right now, we'll sort of assume that we are going to be refining an existing piece of software.

88
00:06:16,340 --> 00:06:20,240
So let's look briefly at the design of the existing software.

89
00:06:20,980 --> 00:06:24,600
So this is a very high-level view.

90
00:06:26,180 --> 00:06:26,240
And...

91
00:06:27,660 --> 00:06:32,180
In this very high-level view, we can see that we have an object called a cargo,

92
00:06:32,640 --> 00:06:37,860
which is populated with all the information that the clerk would collect as they talk to the customer,

93
00:06:38,360 --> 00:06:41,040
especially the origin and destination of that cargo.

94
00:06:42,680 --> 00:06:48,260
Then they pass that information to a service called the routing service.

95
00:06:48,260 --> 00:06:53,260
And that service will figure out the steps, right?

96
00:06:53,360 --> 00:06:57,380
Which actual vessel voyages which ships at which times.

97
00:06:57,660 --> 00:07:02,100
Can actually take the cargo from its origin to its destination.

98
00:07:02,980 --> 00:07:09,680
And as it does that, it puts its answers into a database table called cargo bookings.

99
00:07:10,640 --> 00:07:18,880
Now, this design, which is fairly typical, I think, of legacy software of a certain generation.

100
00:07:19,600 --> 00:07:21,620
And it's not a bad design.

101
00:07:21,620 --> 00:07:27,620
It has one characteristic that's quite good, which is I can explain it at a high level very quickly.

102
00:07:27,660 --> 00:07:28,640
And clearly.

103
00:07:29,380 --> 00:07:33,720
So I think everyone understands this high-level view at this point.

104
00:07:34,060 --> 00:07:35,400
That's a good characteristic.

105
00:07:35,580 --> 00:07:36,820
Not all designs have that.

106
00:07:37,440 --> 00:07:39,300
But it also has some drawbacks.

107
00:07:40,660 --> 00:07:48,760
And some of those drawbacks might be preventing us from adding features or improving features that we want to do.

108
00:07:49,840 --> 00:07:55,980
Now, when I look at a software design, I try to have two perspectives.

109
00:07:55,980 --> 00:07:56,980
And I...

110
00:07:57,660 --> 00:08:01,660
I try to shift back and forth between those two perspectives.

111
00:08:01,660 --> 00:08:07,660
One perspective is the software designer, software engineer type of perspective.

112
00:08:07,660 --> 00:08:21,660
This one, I apply all the principles that, you know, that we have learned about how to make software somewhat clean and changeable and so forth.

113
00:08:21,660 --> 00:08:26,660
For example, with this set of eyes, I might notice that...

114
00:08:26,660 --> 00:08:27,640
that...

115
00:08:27,660 --> 00:08:33,660
there is a side effect here, kind of a problematic side effect.

116
00:08:33,660 --> 00:08:36,660
That as the routing service does its job,

117
00:08:37,660 --> 00:08:42,660
a very complex piece of logic is also updating the database.

118
00:08:43,660 --> 00:08:56,660
To combine complex logic with a database update is basically a violation of an old principle of the separation of the logic from updating state.

119
00:08:56,660 --> 00:08:57,160
And that's...

120
00:08:57,660 --> 00:09:03,660
a principle that goes back before domain-driven design, as a name, anyway.

121
00:09:03,660 --> 00:09:07,660
So I would notice that in other things.

122
00:09:07,660 --> 00:09:14,660
But there's another perspective I want to bring to it, which is the domain perspective.

123
00:09:14,660 --> 00:09:25,660
If I were talking about this design with a person who is not a software developer, but instead an expert in shipping, how would I explain it to them?

124
00:09:25,660 --> 00:09:31,660
And it would start out much the same as the way I talked about it with you.

125
00:09:31,660 --> 00:09:38,660
I would explain how you give the routing service an origin, destination, and a few other things.

126
00:09:38,660 --> 00:09:45,660
And then it will come up with the stops that the cargo will have to make and put them in the database.

127
00:09:45,660 --> 00:09:50,660
And at this level, communication is pretty good.

128
00:09:50,660 --> 00:09:52,660
A shipping expert would understand that.

129
00:09:52,660 --> 00:09:54,660
Would understand these kind of components of a database.

130
00:09:54,660 --> 00:09:58,660
Components of a software system and how they interact.

131
00:09:58,660 --> 00:10:02,660
But we need to get a little more detailed than that.

132
00:10:02,660 --> 00:10:05,660
We can't always have such high-level discussions.

133
00:10:05,660 --> 00:10:10,660
Suppose I wanted to discuss an issue that comes up sometimes.

134
00:10:10,660 --> 00:10:17,660
I showed you an itinerary for a shipment that looks like a pretty good one.

135
00:10:17,660 --> 00:10:22,660
But sometimes, for various reasons, you end up with something like this.

136
00:10:22,660 --> 00:10:23,660
So notice that now,

137
00:10:23,660 --> 00:10:28,660
the cargo is unloaded in Long Beach, California,

138
00:10:28,660 --> 00:10:33,660
and then loaded onto the train in Sacramento, California,

139
00:10:33,660 --> 00:10:36,660
which is hundreds of miles away.

140
00:10:36,660 --> 00:10:39,660
So how did it get from Long Beach to Sacramento?

141
00:10:39,660 --> 00:10:41,660
Well, it wouldn't.

142
00:10:41,660 --> 00:10:45,660
If we had an actual itinerary like this in the system,

143
00:10:45,660 --> 00:10:48,660
they would follow the instructions of the itinerary.

144
00:10:48,660 --> 00:10:51,660
When it arrived in Long Beach, they would unload it.

145
00:10:51,660 --> 00:10:53,660
But then when the time came for the people in Sacramento,

146
00:10:53,660 --> 00:10:54,660
to put it on the train,

147
00:10:54,660 --> 00:10:57,660
they would look around for it and couldn't find it.

148
00:10:57,660 --> 00:11:00,660
And then eventually, someone would figure out what had happened.

149
00:11:00,660 --> 00:11:06,660
But what we want is to be able to notice when this happens

150
00:11:06,660 --> 00:11:09,660
and take a corrective action.

151
00:11:09,660 --> 00:11:12,660
And people talk about this.

152
00:11:12,660 --> 00:11:17,660
And they talk about these kind of disconnected itineraries.

153
00:11:17,660 --> 00:11:22,660
But how is it reflected in the software as it exists?

154
00:11:22,660 --> 00:11:24,660
Well, it is there.

155
00:11:24,660 --> 00:11:29,660
We have some relational integrity constraints in the database table.

156
00:11:29,660 --> 00:11:33,660
But when I start talking about this and this solution,

157
00:11:33,660 --> 00:11:37,660
I find that I lose my connection with the domain expert.

158
00:11:37,660 --> 00:11:41,660
I can't talk about this with a shipping expert

159
00:11:41,660 --> 00:11:44,660
who doesn't really understand software that well.

160
00:11:44,660 --> 00:11:49,660
So we have to find another way of dealing with this

161
00:11:49,660 --> 00:11:51,660
and a way that gives us a design

162
00:11:51,660 --> 00:11:58,660
just as clean and effective but maintains that communication.

163
00:11:58,660 --> 00:12:03,660
So in these kinds of combining these two perspectives,

164
00:12:03,660 --> 00:12:07,660
this is how I want to think as I go forward.

165
00:12:07,660 --> 00:12:12,660
Now, I think about what concepts are we missing

166
00:12:12,660 --> 00:12:16,660
that we are using when we talk to the domain expert.

167
00:12:16,660 --> 00:12:19,660
We're almost shifting between two conceptual systems,

168
00:12:19,660 --> 00:12:21,660
between two models.

169
00:12:21,660 --> 00:12:24,660
Two languages.

170
00:12:24,660 --> 00:12:29,660
So to find an alternative,

171
00:12:29,660 --> 00:12:33,660
we need to somehow get into a creative collaboration

172
00:12:33,660 --> 00:12:36,660
with these software experts.

173
00:12:36,660 --> 00:12:40,660
I mean, between the software experts and the domain experts.

174
00:12:40,660 --> 00:12:42,660
And that's not so easy to do.

175
00:12:42,660 --> 00:12:46,660
Sometimes you can get one of these people into a room

176
00:12:46,660 --> 00:12:48,660
and you've got a nice whiteboard

177
00:12:48,660 --> 00:12:50,660
and you say, you know,

178
00:12:50,660 --> 00:12:54,660
like, well, what concepts are we missing here?

179
00:12:54,660 --> 00:12:58,660
And, you know, nothing will happen here.

180
00:12:58,660 --> 00:13:04,660
So the thing is that getting these conversations started

181
00:13:04,660 --> 00:13:06,660
is quite difficult.

182
00:13:06,660 --> 00:13:10,660
I'm going to talk about sort of the things I often do,

183
00:13:10,660 --> 00:13:13,660
kind of tell a little story of a typical way.

184
00:13:13,660 --> 00:13:17,660
But that doesn't mean this is the way it's done.

185
00:13:17,660 --> 00:13:19,660
And in fact, later in the day,

186
00:13:19,660 --> 00:13:23,660
Alberto Brandolini is going to talk about event storming,

187
00:13:23,660 --> 00:13:27,660
which is a technique that is, among other things,

188
00:13:27,660 --> 00:13:32,660
a great way to get a creative collaboration going

189
00:13:32,660 --> 00:13:35,660
between those two kinds of people, I think.

190
00:13:35,660 --> 00:13:42,660
That's, I think, the most exciting aspect of it to me, actually.

191
00:13:42,660 --> 00:13:48,660
But nonetheless, here's kind of a thing that I do.

192
00:13:48,660 --> 00:13:51,660
Just take this as a story about, you know,

193
00:13:51,660 --> 00:13:55,660
how it was done in this particular case.

194
00:13:55,660 --> 00:13:59,660
First of all, I like to go in with an idea or two, you know,

195
00:13:59,660 --> 00:14:05,660
to just kind of start feeding the conversation.

196
00:14:05,660 --> 00:14:09,660
And where do those ideas come from, though?

197
00:14:09,660 --> 00:14:12,660
Well, often, you know, you're just popping with ideas

198
00:14:12,660 --> 00:14:14,660
if you've been working in the domain.

199
00:14:14,660 --> 00:14:17,660
But one of the principles of domain-driven design

200
00:14:17,660 --> 00:14:20,660
is that the language is very important.

201
00:14:20,660 --> 00:14:22,660
And one thing I've noticed

202
00:14:22,660 --> 00:14:25,660
is that when we talk about our software,

203
00:14:25,660 --> 00:14:30,660
we don't use the same language that's actually in the software.

204
00:14:30,660 --> 00:14:32,660
So, for example, here,

205
00:14:32,660 --> 00:14:36,660
let's compare the way I talked about this system,

206
00:14:36,660 --> 00:14:38,660
even at the high level,

207
00:14:38,660 --> 00:14:40,660
with the way it actually works.

208
00:14:40,660 --> 00:14:43,660
So I said,

209
00:14:43,660 --> 00:14:46,660
give the routing service

210
00:14:46,660 --> 00:14:49,660
an origin and a destination arrival time.

211
00:14:49,660 --> 00:14:51,660
And if you look at the diagram,

212
00:14:51,660 --> 00:14:53,660
you will see that, in fact,

213
00:14:53,660 --> 00:14:55,660
that's exactly what happens.

214
00:14:55,660 --> 00:14:57,660
I mean, taking this diagram

215
00:14:57,660 --> 00:15:00,660
to be an accurate representation of the software.

216
00:15:00,660 --> 00:15:02,660
So, so far, so good.

217
00:15:02,660 --> 00:15:05,660
And then I say that the routing service,

218
00:15:05,660 --> 00:15:07,660
it, the routing service,

219
00:15:07,660 --> 00:15:10,660
will look up the stops the cargo will have to make.

220
00:15:10,660 --> 00:15:12,660
Now, the thing about that

221
00:15:12,660 --> 00:15:14,660
is that the word stops

222
00:15:14,660 --> 00:15:16,660
does not appear in the diagram.

223
00:15:16,660 --> 00:15:19,660
And we can do a little search

224
00:15:19,660 --> 00:15:21,660
of the code and so forth,

225
00:15:21,660 --> 00:15:23,660
and we will find that

226
00:15:23,660 --> 00:15:25,660
stops is never used in this way

227
00:15:25,660 --> 00:15:27,660
anywhere in the software.

228
00:15:27,660 --> 00:15:29,660
It might be in a label here or there.

229
00:15:29,660 --> 00:15:31,660
So this is a concept so basic

230
00:15:31,660 --> 00:15:33,660
that I use it to explain

231
00:15:33,660 --> 00:15:35,660
what the routing service does.

232
00:15:35,660 --> 00:15:37,660
What does the routing service do?

233
00:15:37,660 --> 00:15:39,660
It looks up the stops the cargo has to make.

234
00:15:39,660 --> 00:15:41,660
But that's not actually

235
00:15:41,660 --> 00:15:43,660
what's happening in the software.

236
00:15:43,660 --> 00:15:45,660
Not at least in those words.

237
00:15:45,660 --> 00:15:47,660
So maybe here we have an idea

238
00:15:47,660 --> 00:15:50,660
that could feed the conversation.

239
00:15:50,660 --> 00:15:52,660
And so I could propose something.

240
00:15:52,660 --> 00:15:54,660
I could say, well, cargo,

241
00:15:54,660 --> 00:15:56,660
we already have the cargo object.

242
00:15:56,660 --> 00:15:58,660
Maybe the cargo object

243
00:15:58,660 --> 00:16:00,660
could have a series of stops.

244
00:16:00,660 --> 00:16:03,660
Now, I propose this,

245
00:16:03,660 --> 00:16:06,660
and my shipping counterpart says,

246
00:16:06,660 --> 00:16:08,660
yeah, that sounds good.

247
00:16:08,660 --> 00:16:10,660
Let's do it that way.

248
00:16:10,660 --> 00:16:12,660
And so I feel good

249
00:16:12,660 --> 00:16:14,660
that I had a good idea.

250
00:16:14,660 --> 00:16:16,660
Now we can take this.

251
00:16:16,660 --> 00:16:18,660
We can refactor the software.

252
00:16:18,660 --> 00:16:20,660
We can have a more,

253
00:16:20,660 --> 00:16:22,660
a better communication

254
00:16:22,660 --> 00:16:24,660
between the two types of people

255
00:16:24,660 --> 00:16:27,660
and so forth and never look back.

256
00:16:27,660 --> 00:16:30,660
Actually, this is often what happens.

257
00:16:30,660 --> 00:16:33,660
And that is another point

258
00:16:33,660 --> 00:16:35,660
where things can go awry

259
00:16:35,660 --> 00:16:37,660
because at this point,

260
00:16:37,660 --> 00:16:39,660
I will have lost a great opportunity.

261
00:16:39,660 --> 00:16:42,660
The first decent idea that came along

262
00:16:42,660 --> 00:16:44,660
is often the last idea.

263
00:16:44,660 --> 00:16:47,660
Because we latch onto it

264
00:16:47,660 --> 00:16:50,660
and we stop thinking.

265
00:16:50,660 --> 00:16:53,660
As a metaphor for this,

266
00:16:53,660 --> 00:16:56,660
or a comparison anyway,

267
00:16:56,660 --> 00:16:58,660
think of what you would do

268
00:16:58,660 --> 00:17:01,660
if you decided to go shopping for a car.

269
00:17:01,660 --> 00:17:03,660
You might do a little research

270
00:17:03,660 --> 00:17:05,660
and you'd say, okay, what do I need?

271
00:17:05,660 --> 00:17:07,660
And you write down some things you need.

272
00:17:07,660 --> 00:17:10,660
I want to have a sedan

273
00:17:10,660 --> 00:17:12,660
with enough room for four people

274
00:17:12,660 --> 00:17:14,660
and so forth.

275
00:17:14,660 --> 00:17:16,660
And so you go,

276
00:17:16,660 --> 00:17:18,660
and I'd like to have it be green.

277
00:17:18,660 --> 00:17:20,660
And so you go to a dealer

278
00:17:20,660 --> 00:17:22,660
and you say, well, these are things I want.

279
00:17:22,660 --> 00:17:24,660
I want a four-door sedan.

280
00:17:24,660 --> 00:17:26,660
I want it to be green.

281
00:17:26,660 --> 00:17:28,660
And there are other constraints there too.

282
00:17:28,660 --> 00:17:30,660
So the dealer says, all right,

283
00:17:30,660 --> 00:17:32,660
well, here's a car over here

284
00:17:32,660 --> 00:17:34,660
that is green, four doors.

285
00:17:34,660 --> 00:17:36,660
It meets all your requirements.

286
00:17:36,660 --> 00:17:38,660
And so you say, okay, I'll take it.

287
00:17:38,660 --> 00:17:41,660
Is that how you shop for a car?

288
00:17:41,660 --> 00:17:43,660
Now, what you really do probably

289
00:17:43,660 --> 00:17:45,660
is you say, okay, well,

290
00:17:45,660 --> 00:17:47,660
let me test drive that car.

291
00:17:47,660 --> 00:17:49,660
And then I'm going to go and shop around

292
00:17:49,660 --> 00:17:51,660
at some other cars too.

293
00:17:51,660 --> 00:17:53,660
And then in the end,

294
00:17:53,660 --> 00:17:55,660
it's actually possible that I will come back

295
00:17:55,660 --> 00:17:57,660
and buy that car.

296
00:17:57,660 --> 00:17:59,660
But I would not feel good about buying that car

297
00:17:59,660 --> 00:18:01,660
having not looked at any others.

298
00:18:01,660 --> 00:18:03,660
But what's the difference between buying a car

299
00:18:03,660 --> 00:18:05,660
and designing a piece of software?

300
00:18:05,660 --> 00:18:08,660
I can only think of two real differences.

301
00:18:08,660 --> 00:18:12,660
One is that the possibilities for software

302
00:18:12,660 --> 00:18:15,660
are much broader and less well understood.

303
00:18:15,660 --> 00:18:17,660
So if I actually did buy that car

304
00:18:17,660 --> 00:18:19,660
or indeed pick a car at random,

305
00:18:19,660 --> 00:18:22,660
the chances are that it would suit most of my needs.

306
00:18:22,660 --> 00:18:24,660
That's the truth.

307
00:18:24,660 --> 00:18:27,660
In 2019, I could pick a car at random

308
00:18:27,660 --> 00:18:30,660
and it would suit 90% of the needs that I have.

309
00:18:30,660 --> 00:18:34,660
That's probably not true of a software design.

310
00:18:34,660 --> 00:18:37,660
The other thing is that most of the time

311
00:18:37,660 --> 00:18:40,660
software development is quite a bit more expensive

312
00:18:40,660 --> 00:18:42,660
than buying a car.

313
00:18:42,660 --> 00:18:44,660
So both of those, though,

314
00:18:44,660 --> 00:18:46,660
would suggest spending more effort

315
00:18:46,660 --> 00:18:48,660
trying to choose a software model

316
00:18:48,660 --> 00:18:50,660
than we do a car.

317
00:18:50,660 --> 00:18:52,660
And specifically I mean

318
00:18:52,660 --> 00:18:55,660
looking at more alternatives.

319
00:18:55,660 --> 00:18:57,660
Because there are lots of methods

320
00:18:57,660 --> 00:18:59,660
where people put a lot of energy

321
00:18:59,660 --> 00:19:01,660
into designing the software.

322
00:19:01,660 --> 00:19:04,660
But that energy doesn't usually go to

323
00:19:04,660 --> 00:19:07,660
looking at a broad range of alternatives.

324
00:19:07,660 --> 00:19:10,660
So somehow or other,

325
00:19:10,660 --> 00:19:12,660
we've got to get some alternatives in there.

326
00:19:12,660 --> 00:19:15,660
And if I've been listening to the way people talk,

327
00:19:15,660 --> 00:19:18,660
I realize that some people sometimes say

328
00:19:18,660 --> 00:19:23,660
that the cargo's itinerary is a series of stops.

329
00:19:23,660 --> 00:19:25,660
But sometimes they say

330
00:19:25,660 --> 00:19:28,660
that it's made up of a sequence of legs.

331
00:19:28,660 --> 00:19:30,660
So the cargo will...

332
00:19:30,660 --> 00:19:32,660
So a leg will be like, you know,

333
00:19:32,660 --> 00:19:34,660
the part that goes from Hong Kong

334
00:19:34,660 --> 00:19:36,660
to Long Beach, California

335
00:19:36,660 --> 00:19:38,660
on a particular ship.

336
00:19:38,660 --> 00:19:40,660
That's one leg.

337
00:19:40,660 --> 00:19:43,660
So maybe we should try that out.

338
00:19:43,660 --> 00:19:47,660
So I'm going to throw that up there, too.

339
00:19:47,660 --> 00:19:49,660
Now this time,

340
00:19:49,660 --> 00:19:51,660
the conversation has to change.

341
00:19:51,660 --> 00:19:54,660
The shipping expert can't just say,

342
00:19:54,660 --> 00:19:56,660
that's good, do that.

343
00:19:56,660 --> 00:19:58,660
Of course, if legs had come first,

344
00:19:58,660 --> 00:20:00,660
that's what would have happened.

345
00:20:00,660 --> 00:20:03,660
But now it's more of a conversation about,

346
00:20:03,660 --> 00:20:06,660
well, why should I use one versus the other?

347
00:20:06,660 --> 00:20:09,660
Or maybe it will even trigger...

348
00:20:09,660 --> 00:20:11,660
I find that about three,

349
00:20:11,660 --> 00:20:13,660
if you can get three,

350
00:20:13,660 --> 00:20:16,660
it changes the way people think

351
00:20:16,660 --> 00:20:20,660
from sort of choosing one

352
00:20:20,660 --> 00:20:24,660
to realizing that they're just looking at examples

353
00:20:24,660 --> 00:20:26,660
from a very large set.

354
00:20:26,660 --> 00:20:28,660
And if you can get to that point

355
00:20:28,660 --> 00:20:30,660
where you have that shift in your mind,

356
00:20:30,660 --> 00:20:32,660
where you realize you're looking at samples

357
00:20:32,660 --> 00:20:34,660
from a very large set,

358
00:20:34,660 --> 00:20:36,660
it gets to be much easier

359
00:20:36,660 --> 00:20:38,660
to come up with more alternatives.

360
00:20:38,660 --> 00:20:42,660
But it's not so easy to get those first three.

361
00:20:42,660 --> 00:20:45,660
I can do my little trick again.

362
00:20:45,660 --> 00:20:47,660
So, for example,

363
00:20:47,660 --> 00:20:49,660
what I said earlier

364
00:20:49,660 --> 00:20:54,660
is that a cargo's itinerary is a series of legs,

365
00:20:54,660 --> 00:20:58,660
and I explained what a leg is.

366
00:20:58,660 --> 00:21:02,660
But let's just ignore the bubble on the right.

367
00:21:02,660 --> 00:21:04,660
Do the trick of comparing what I say

368
00:21:04,660 --> 00:21:06,660
to the more formal...

369
00:21:06,660 --> 00:21:08,660
In this case, it's just a diagram on a board.

370
00:21:08,660 --> 00:21:10,660
It's not software, but still,

371
00:21:10,660 --> 00:21:13,660
the diagram on the board has already diverged

372
00:21:13,660 --> 00:21:16,660
from the way I talk about it.

373
00:21:16,660 --> 00:21:19,660
So look at that for a moment.

374
00:21:19,660 --> 00:21:21,660
What is the difference?

375
00:21:21,660 --> 00:21:26,660
Can someone just point out a difference, any difference?

376
00:21:26,660 --> 00:21:28,660
Location of the bubble.

377
00:21:28,660 --> 00:21:30,660
The...

378
00:21:30,660 --> 00:21:31,660
So the words, I mean,

379
00:21:31,660 --> 00:21:34,660
how do the words not correspond to the diagram?

380
00:21:34,660 --> 00:21:36,660
A cargo's itinerary is a series of legs,

381
00:21:36,660 --> 00:21:38,660
and I have that diagram,

382
00:21:38,660 --> 00:21:40,660
and that's number one.

383
00:21:42,660 --> 00:21:44,660
So there are two differences,

384
00:21:44,660 --> 00:21:47,660
and I think I heard them both come from various people.

385
00:21:47,660 --> 00:21:50,660
One difference is that the diagram is less specific.

386
00:21:50,660 --> 00:21:52,660
It doesn't say that the legs are a series.

387
00:21:52,660 --> 00:21:54,660
It just says it's a collection.

388
00:21:54,660 --> 00:21:56,660
So we could fix that.

389
00:21:56,660 --> 00:21:58,660
And that's kind of a detail, though.

390
00:21:58,660 --> 00:22:00,660
I mean to say it would be a good thing to fix,

391
00:22:00,660 --> 00:22:02,660
but it isn't a fundamental change

392
00:22:02,660 --> 00:22:04,660
because a series is a collection.

393
00:22:04,660 --> 00:22:06,660
But the thing that really is missing

394
00:22:06,660 --> 00:22:08,660
is the word itinerary.

395
00:22:08,660 --> 00:22:10,660
That is, the statement doesn't say

396
00:22:10,660 --> 00:22:12,660
a cargo has legs.

397
00:22:12,660 --> 00:22:14,660
In fact, as an English statement,

398
00:22:14,660 --> 00:22:16,660
that sounds very weird.

399
00:22:16,660 --> 00:22:17,660
A cargo has legs.

400
00:22:17,660 --> 00:22:19,660
What sort of cargo has legs?

401
00:22:19,660 --> 00:22:22,660
Maybe we're transporting live animals or something.

402
00:22:22,660 --> 00:22:25,660
Or chairs.

403
00:22:25,660 --> 00:22:27,660
Yes, I'm transporting chairs,

404
00:22:27,660 --> 00:22:29,660
and my cargo has legs.

405
00:22:29,660 --> 00:22:32,660
Well, that's not what this means, though, right?

406
00:22:32,660 --> 00:22:34,660
That's a different sense of legs.

407
00:22:34,660 --> 00:22:36,660
So we need a...

408
00:22:36,660 --> 00:22:39,660
We need to really take seriously

409
00:22:39,660 --> 00:22:42,660
that we're not using the same language.

410
00:22:45,660 --> 00:22:48,660
And here's an observation I want to make.

411
00:22:48,660 --> 00:22:50,660
I've used the word itinerary

412
00:22:50,660 --> 00:22:54,660
in this presentation many times.

413
00:22:54,660 --> 00:22:57,660
But I don't think people were sitting there saying,

414
00:22:57,660 --> 00:22:59,660
well, why isn't he putting itinerary up

415
00:22:59,660 --> 00:23:02,660
as a concept to use in our model?

416
00:23:02,660 --> 00:23:04,660
You weren't thinking that.

417
00:23:04,660 --> 00:23:06,660
Nobody would think that

418
00:23:06,660 --> 00:23:09,660
because we're just used to hearing words,

419
00:23:09,660 --> 00:23:12,660
and, you know, we get the gist of what's being said.

420
00:23:12,660 --> 00:23:14,660
You get the meaning of what I'm saying,

421
00:23:14,660 --> 00:23:16,660
but not paying enough attention

422
00:23:16,660 --> 00:23:18,660
to the actual words themselves.

423
00:23:18,660 --> 00:23:20,660
And I think that if you pay attention

424
00:23:20,660 --> 00:23:22,660
to the words themselves,

425
00:23:22,660 --> 00:23:24,660
it gives you wonderful clues

426
00:23:24,660 --> 00:23:27,660
to models that can really help

427
00:23:27,660 --> 00:23:30,660
solve the problems in the software.

428
00:23:30,660 --> 00:23:34,660
So I'm going to add a couple more.

429
00:23:34,660 --> 00:23:35,660
One would be that a cargo

430
00:23:35,660 --> 00:23:37,660
would have an itinerary,

431
00:23:37,660 --> 00:23:39,660
and that itinerary would have a sequence of legs.

432
00:23:39,660 --> 00:23:41,660
And another would be that it would have

433
00:23:41,660 --> 00:23:42,660
a sequence of stops.

434
00:23:42,660 --> 00:23:44,660
I could do it both ways.

435
00:23:44,660 --> 00:23:47,660
So now I've got at least four distinct models

436
00:23:47,660 --> 00:23:52,660
and minor variations around those.

437
00:23:52,660 --> 00:23:54,660
And at this point, hopefully,

438
00:23:54,660 --> 00:23:57,660
we would have other ideas as well.

439
00:23:57,660 --> 00:23:59,660
But that's enough to get, I think,

440
00:23:59,660 --> 00:24:02,660
the sense of what I'm trying to do here.

441
00:24:02,660 --> 00:24:04,660
And notice that these diagrams are written

442
00:24:04,660 --> 00:24:09,660
in a very informal style.

443
00:24:09,660 --> 00:24:12,660
The point being that we don't want to invest a lot

444
00:24:12,660 --> 00:24:14,660
in these ideas at this stage.

445
00:24:14,660 --> 00:24:16,660
We want quantity,

446
00:24:16,660 --> 00:24:20,660
but not depth of analysis.

447
00:24:20,660 --> 00:24:23,660
We can do the depth of analysis later if we need to,

448
00:24:23,660 --> 00:24:26,660
but right now we want to get broad.

449
00:24:26,660 --> 00:24:28,660
That means, in fact, we need some ideas.

450
00:24:28,660 --> 00:24:30,660
We don't really have any in this picture,

451
00:24:30,660 --> 00:24:33,660
but we need some ideas that are just bad.

452
00:24:33,660 --> 00:24:36,660
You don't know if you've really explored

453
00:24:36,660 --> 00:24:38,660
the space of the problem,

454
00:24:38,660 --> 00:24:41,660
or rather the space of possible solutions,

455
00:24:41,660 --> 00:24:44,660
if you never come up with a bad idea.

456
00:24:44,660 --> 00:24:50,660
But because when I made this slide,

457
00:24:50,660 --> 00:24:54,660
I was too familiar with the problem, perhaps,

458
00:24:54,660 --> 00:24:57,660
or just too embarrassed to show a really bad idea,

459
00:24:57,660 --> 00:25:02,660
this isn't a good illustration of that principle.

460
00:25:03,660 --> 00:25:07,660
All right, so there's one of the things.

461
00:25:07,660 --> 00:25:09,660
You've got to generate some variation,

462
00:25:09,660 --> 00:25:12,660
but you've also got to keep moving.

463
00:25:12,660 --> 00:25:17,660
One of the things that happens to people doing software modeling

464
00:25:17,660 --> 00:25:20,660
is that they get, and this is the perfectionism again,

465
00:25:20,660 --> 00:25:24,660
they get into a loop of just doing more and more,

466
00:25:24,660 --> 00:25:26,660
spending time.

467
00:25:26,660 --> 00:25:28,660
If you're not getting good ideas,

468
00:25:28,660 --> 00:25:31,660
then move on to something else, come back to it later.

469
00:25:31,660 --> 00:25:32,660
And don't be too finicky.

470
00:25:32,660 --> 00:25:35,660
You know, I don't think you can really succeed in this

471
00:25:35,660 --> 00:25:39,660
if you have a group of people that's working on it

472
00:25:39,660 --> 00:25:41,660
where you will really be,

473
00:25:41,660 --> 00:25:44,660
where your prestige will be lowered

474
00:25:44,660 --> 00:25:48,660
by you putting up a bad idea.

475
00:25:48,660 --> 00:25:51,660
If you put up a model and everyone says,

476
00:25:51,660 --> 00:25:56,660
well, look, it's got the wrong kind of dependencies in it.

477
00:25:56,660 --> 00:25:59,660
And then you say, oh, well, I'm sorry,

478
00:25:59,660 --> 00:26:01,660
I'll go away and never talk again.

479
00:26:01,660 --> 00:26:06,660
If you're in that kind of place, this isn't going to work.

480
00:26:06,660 --> 00:26:12,660
So, now also implicitly I think I've said

481
00:26:12,660 --> 00:26:15,660
that there would be more than one session like this.

482
00:26:15,660 --> 00:26:19,660
I don't like these sessions to run on for hours and hours.

483
00:26:19,660 --> 00:26:21,660
An hour and a half is long enough.

484
00:26:21,660 --> 00:26:23,660
At that point you're not going to have any new ideas.

485
00:26:23,660 --> 00:26:25,660
So you have to have multiple sessions.

486
00:26:25,660 --> 00:26:27,660
And then people get to the question,

487
00:26:27,660 --> 00:26:29,660
well, what do you document about this?

488
00:26:29,660 --> 00:26:31,660
This is meant to be low cost.

489
00:26:31,660 --> 00:26:36,660
So I would say that I would just look at the board and say,

490
00:26:36,660 --> 00:26:38,660
is there anything here worth remembering

491
00:26:38,660 --> 00:26:41,660
that we would want to feed into the next session?

492
00:26:41,660 --> 00:26:44,660
And the answer might be, no, it was a good discussion,

493
00:26:44,660 --> 00:26:46,660
but we don't need to keep any of this.

494
00:26:46,660 --> 00:26:48,660
And then you can just forget about it

495
00:26:48,660 --> 00:26:52,660
and schedule the next meeting and be done.

496
00:26:52,660 --> 00:26:55,660
Here I think we did get some things.

497
00:26:55,660 --> 00:26:58,660
I would just say those three words would be sufficient.

498
00:26:58,660 --> 00:27:01,660
But maybe the diagram or the cargo itinerary statement

499
00:27:01,660 --> 00:27:03,660
would be good to have.

500
00:27:03,660 --> 00:27:06,660
That would be enough.

501
00:27:06,660 --> 00:27:09,660
And I might write those in my design notebook

502
00:27:09,660 --> 00:27:12,660
and just leave it at that.

503
00:27:12,660 --> 00:27:19,660
We don't have to have a wiki documenting every idea that we have.

504
00:27:19,660 --> 00:27:21,660
And now we have to move on.

505
00:27:21,660 --> 00:27:25,660
Now, at some point you're going to have to decide, right?

506
00:27:25,660 --> 00:27:27,660
Generating variation is very important,

507
00:27:27,660 --> 00:27:30,660
but then you have to select from the variation.

508
00:27:30,660 --> 00:27:33,660
So it's a very different kind of thinking,

509
00:27:33,660 --> 00:27:38,660
different mode of thought and also different criteria,

510
00:27:38,660 --> 00:27:41,660
and I think different people.

511
00:27:41,660 --> 00:27:43,660
So one thing that I think is important, too,

512
00:27:43,660 --> 00:27:45,660
is that you have different meetings,

513
00:27:45,660 --> 00:27:47,660
probably with different people,

514
00:27:47,660 --> 00:27:51,660
to come up with the ideas or to select

515
00:27:51,660 --> 00:27:56,660
or, most importantly, to decide whether to actually use something.

516
00:27:56,660 --> 00:27:59,660
So that's really, I guess, three different kinds of things.

517
00:27:59,660 --> 00:28:00,660
But the most important thing,

518
00:28:00,660 --> 00:28:03,660
the most important is when it comes time to actually decide

519
00:28:03,660 --> 00:28:04,660
and we're thinking about,

520
00:28:04,660 --> 00:28:08,660
oh, should we actually do this thing,

521
00:28:08,660 --> 00:28:11,660
be sure to have a meeting where the stakeholders

522
00:28:11,660 --> 00:28:15,660
that would be affected are there.

523
00:28:15,660 --> 00:28:19,660
That might be the project manager.

524
00:28:19,660 --> 00:28:24,660
It might be another project manager from another team

525
00:28:24,660 --> 00:28:26,660
where your stuff is used.

526
00:28:26,660 --> 00:28:28,660
If you don't do this,

527
00:28:28,660 --> 00:28:29,660
the next time you try to have idea generating meetings,

528
00:28:29,660 --> 00:28:33,660
you'll have all those people there

529
00:28:33,660 --> 00:28:37,660
and you won't be generating very many ideas.

530
00:28:37,660 --> 00:28:40,660
Well, anyway, how do we choose a model?

531
00:28:40,660 --> 00:28:44,660
And I don't mean forever necessarily, but for now.

532
00:28:44,660 --> 00:28:47,660
So stops or legs, for example.

533
00:28:47,660 --> 00:28:49,660
Let's just boil it down to these two.

534
00:28:49,660 --> 00:28:52,660
We're going to have a cargo with an itinerary with stops

535
00:28:52,660 --> 00:28:54,660
or a cargo with an itinerary with legs

536
00:28:54,660 --> 00:28:56,660
and just have a show of hands.

537
00:28:56,660 --> 00:28:58,660
Who thinks we should choose stops?

538
00:28:59,660 --> 00:29:02,660
And who thinks we should choose legs?

539
00:29:02,660 --> 00:29:04,660
All right, legs wins.

540
00:29:04,660 --> 00:29:06,660
I think this would be a better process

541
00:29:06,660 --> 00:29:09,660
than what we usually do.

542
00:29:09,660 --> 00:29:11,660
What we usually do is we'll have some document

543
00:29:11,660 --> 00:29:13,660
with a nice diagram in it

544
00:29:13,660 --> 00:29:15,660
and then at some future time,

545
00:29:15,660 --> 00:29:17,660
a programmer will get a story card

546
00:29:17,660 --> 00:29:21,660
and it will have, you know,

547
00:29:21,660 --> 00:29:23,660
require some of this functionality

548
00:29:23,660 --> 00:29:26,660
and they'll do it some way.

549
00:29:26,660 --> 00:29:27,660
That's about it.

550
00:29:27,660 --> 00:29:29,660
Or maybe they'll get the diagram,

551
00:29:29,660 --> 00:29:32,660
but with no kind of meaning.

552
00:29:32,660 --> 00:29:34,660
And so they'll make those objects.

553
00:29:34,660 --> 00:29:40,660
But none of the sort of kind of subtlety

554
00:29:40,660 --> 00:29:44,660
of the concepts will come through.

555
00:29:44,660 --> 00:29:46,660
So we can do better than that.

556
00:29:46,660 --> 00:29:48,660
But before we do, I want to talk briefly

557
00:29:48,660 --> 00:29:50,660
about what I mean when I say model.

558
00:29:50,660 --> 00:29:52,660
Because I've used the word a lot of times,

559
00:29:52,660 --> 00:29:54,660
but I haven't really said what I mean.

560
00:29:54,660 --> 00:29:56,660
And there are a lot of meanings to the word model

561
00:29:56,660 --> 00:29:58,660
and you guys know many of them.

562
00:29:58,660 --> 00:30:00,660
You can eliminate some of the obvious ones.

563
00:30:00,660 --> 00:30:03,660
Don't mean a tiny ship in a bottle.

564
00:30:03,660 --> 00:30:07,660
But there are, for example,

565
00:30:07,660 --> 00:30:09,660
people often say model

566
00:30:09,660 --> 00:30:11,660
and they mean a UML diagram.

567
00:30:11,660 --> 00:30:14,660
And I've used a number of UML diagrams,

568
00:30:14,660 --> 00:30:16,660
but that's not what I mean.

569
00:30:16,660 --> 00:30:18,660
What I really mean is the concept

570
00:30:18,660 --> 00:30:21,660
behind that diagram.

571
00:30:21,660 --> 00:30:23,660
So if we look at a map of the world,

572
00:30:23,660 --> 00:30:25,660
we could say that this is a kind of model

573
00:30:25,660 --> 00:30:27,660
of the world.

574
00:30:27,660 --> 00:30:29,660
And yet, and it is,

575
00:30:29,660 --> 00:30:33,660
but still it's not quite the kind of model I mean.

576
00:30:33,660 --> 00:30:36,660
I mean the model behind this map.

577
00:30:36,660 --> 00:30:39,660
So take a quick look at the map

578
00:30:39,660 --> 00:30:41,660
and you can see things like,

579
00:30:41,660 --> 00:30:43,660
I don't know, how big things are.

580
00:30:43,660 --> 00:30:45,660
You could say the United States and China

581
00:30:45,660 --> 00:30:47,660
look about the same size.

582
00:30:47,660 --> 00:30:51,660
And they actually are approximately the same size.

583
00:30:51,660 --> 00:30:53,660
Or you could look and see that Greenland and Africa

584
00:30:53,660 --> 00:30:55,660
are about the same size.

585
00:30:55,660 --> 00:30:57,660
So I suppose by extrapolation,

586
00:30:57,660 --> 00:31:00,660
that they are the same size.

587
00:31:00,660 --> 00:31:03,660
Oh, I see someone shaking their head.

588
00:31:03,660 --> 00:31:05,660
So apparently they're not the same size.

589
00:31:05,660 --> 00:31:08,660
Here's a different map of the world.

590
00:31:08,660 --> 00:31:11,660
This one shows Africa and Greenland.

591
00:31:11,660 --> 00:31:14,660
And oh, I guess they're not quite the same size.

592
00:31:14,660 --> 00:31:18,660
In fact, Africa is 14 times bigger than Greenland.

593
00:31:18,660 --> 00:31:20,660
But they look the same size on that map.

594
00:31:20,660 --> 00:31:25,660
Now is that because the people who made that map

595
00:31:25,660 --> 00:31:29,660
didn't know how big Greenland was?

596
00:31:29,660 --> 00:31:32,660
Or perhaps they thought,

597
00:31:32,660 --> 00:31:34,660
you know, this is my pet theory

598
00:31:34,660 --> 00:31:39,660
that got shot down by this XKCD comic.

599
00:31:39,660 --> 00:31:43,660
But my pet theory was that this map was made in the US.

600
00:31:43,660 --> 00:31:47,660
You can tell because it's right in the middle of the map.

601
00:31:47,660 --> 00:31:50,660
And so you know where it was made.

602
00:31:50,660 --> 00:31:54,660
And then there were some immigrants

603
00:31:54,660 --> 00:31:58,660
on the team that made the map from Greenland.

604
00:31:58,660 --> 00:32:01,660
And they said, Americans aren't very good at geography.

605
00:32:01,660 --> 00:32:04,660
And if we make Greenland huge,

606
00:32:04,660 --> 00:32:09,660
generations of Americans will think that Greenland is big.

607
00:32:09,660 --> 00:32:12,660
And so that's what happened.

608
00:32:12,660 --> 00:32:15,660
But actually it turns out that's not what happened.

609
00:32:15,660 --> 00:32:19,660
The Mercator projection is the reason.

610
00:32:19,660 --> 00:32:22,660
Now, why though did the Mercator projection

611
00:32:22,660 --> 00:32:24,660
become so extremely popular?

612
00:32:24,660 --> 00:32:27,660
That turns out to be because it was very useful

613
00:32:27,660 --> 00:32:30,660
for a particular case which was important

614
00:32:30,660 --> 00:32:33,660
in the 1590s when it was made,

615
00:32:33,660 --> 00:32:36,660
which is that if you make a map based on this projection,

616
00:32:36,660 --> 00:32:39,660
you can draw a line between two points.

617
00:32:39,660 --> 00:32:42,660
And that line, the angle of that line

618
00:32:42,660 --> 00:32:44,660
corresponds to a compass heading.

619
00:32:44,660 --> 00:32:48,660
If you want to know what direction, you know,

620
00:32:48,660 --> 00:32:51,660
New York is from Lisbon,

621
00:32:51,660 --> 00:32:53,660
you draw a line between New York and Lisbon.

622
00:32:53,660 --> 00:32:56,660
And if you pointed a ship that direction

623
00:32:56,660 --> 00:32:57,660
and went in a straight line,

624
00:32:57,660 --> 00:32:59,660
of course you wouldn't do that,

625
00:32:59,660 --> 00:33:03,660
but it's a sort of starting point for a navigator.

626
00:33:03,660 --> 00:33:08,660
So this map was really made for use on ships by navigators.

627
00:33:08,660 --> 00:33:12,660
But then it was good enough to use for many other purposes.

628
00:33:12,660 --> 00:33:16,660
And sometimes you can even tell things like how big something is.

629
00:33:16,660 --> 00:33:21,660
You can tell that the US and China are about the same size

630
00:33:21,660 --> 00:33:23,660
because they're at about the same height.

631
00:33:23,660 --> 00:33:26,660
They're about the same latitude.

632
00:33:26,660 --> 00:33:28,660
As long as something's at the same latitude,

633
00:33:28,660 --> 00:33:30,660
you can compare the size.

634
00:33:30,660 --> 00:33:32,660
If you wanted to,

635
00:33:32,660 --> 00:33:35,660
you could compare the size of Africa and Greenland

636
00:33:35,660 --> 00:33:38,660
by making an adjustment based on the latitude.

637
00:33:38,660 --> 00:33:41,660
It would be a more complicated calculation.

638
00:33:41,660 --> 00:33:44,660
So by choosing a model that is well suited

639
00:33:44,660 --> 00:33:47,660
for a particular problem you want to solve,

640
00:33:47,660 --> 00:33:52,660
you can make the computation simpler and more complex.

641
00:33:52,660 --> 00:33:56,660
This one makes figuring out directions very simple,

642
00:33:56,660 --> 00:34:00,660
but it makes figuring out the size of things a bit complex.

643
00:34:00,660 --> 00:34:02,660
But it's still possible to do.

644
00:34:02,660 --> 00:34:06,660
If I wanted to know the population of the US versus China,

645
00:34:06,660 --> 00:34:09,660
I could not do that with this map,

646
00:34:09,660 --> 00:34:12,660
no matter how complicated the calculation was.

647
00:34:12,660 --> 00:34:15,660
So there's these sort of factors.

648
00:34:15,660 --> 00:34:20,660
Now, what is the model as I mean it?

649
00:34:20,660 --> 00:34:21,660
Well,

650
00:34:21,660 --> 00:34:23,660
behind this model,

651
00:34:23,660 --> 00:34:25,660
behind that map rather,

652
00:34:25,660 --> 00:34:28,660
is the concept that the world is round.

653
00:34:28,660 --> 00:34:30,660
It's implicit in it,

654
00:34:30,660 --> 00:34:32,660
but that the world is round

655
00:34:32,660 --> 00:34:35,660
and that all we're interested in is the surface

656
00:34:35,660 --> 00:34:37,660
and certain points on that surface.

657
00:34:37,660 --> 00:34:40,660
We're not interested in the chemical makeup of the land

658
00:34:40,660 --> 00:34:44,660
or anything about the magma underneath.

659
00:34:44,660 --> 00:34:47,660
We're interested in where the coastlines are.

660
00:34:47,660 --> 00:34:49,660
So we need a way of describing that,

661
00:34:49,660 --> 00:34:51,660
a spherical coordinate system.

662
00:34:51,660 --> 00:34:53,660
Such as latitude and longitude.

663
00:34:53,660 --> 00:34:57,660
Then we transform all our data into that form.

664
00:34:57,660 --> 00:35:01,660
Then we need to convert it into a flat map,

665
00:35:01,660 --> 00:35:03,660
or technically into a cylinder,

666
00:35:03,660 --> 00:35:07,660
because you could rotate that map.

667
00:35:07,660 --> 00:35:11,660
And we need to do this in a way,

668
00:35:11,660 --> 00:35:13,660
because there are an infinite number of ways

669
00:35:13,660 --> 00:35:17,660
of transforming a spherical surface into a cylinder,

670
00:35:17,660 --> 00:35:19,660
but we need to do it in a way

671
00:35:19,660 --> 00:35:21,660
that preserves the direction

672
00:35:21,660 --> 00:35:23,660
of two points relative to each other.

673
00:35:23,660 --> 00:35:25,660
There's only one way to do that,

674
00:35:25,660 --> 00:35:27,660
and it's the Mercator projection.

675
00:35:27,660 --> 00:35:30,660
This is the model.

676
00:35:30,660 --> 00:35:33,660
And if you take that model,

677
00:35:33,660 --> 00:35:36,660
I mean, if you look at the pieces of it,

678
00:35:36,660 --> 00:35:38,660
you see that here's an abstraction.

679
00:35:38,660 --> 00:35:41,660
This very complex and concrete thing, the Earth,

680
00:35:41,660 --> 00:35:46,660
is abstracted as this simple geographical figure.

681
00:35:46,660 --> 00:35:49,660
And we aren't using all the data we have.

682
00:35:49,660 --> 00:35:51,660
We're using certain kinds of data.

683
00:35:51,660 --> 00:35:54,660
In this case, the location of coastlines

684
00:35:54,660 --> 00:35:57,660
and rivers and so on.

685
00:35:57,660 --> 00:36:00,660
Using another abstraction to represent it

686
00:36:00,660 --> 00:36:03,660
relative to the other.

687
00:36:03,660 --> 00:36:06,660
That is, latitude and longitude is meaningful

688
00:36:06,660 --> 00:36:09,660
in relation to a sphere.

689
00:36:09,660 --> 00:36:13,660
Then we need some math to do the transformation.

690
00:36:13,660 --> 00:36:16,660
Now, I don't know exactly how Mercator did it.

691
00:36:16,660 --> 00:36:19,660
Some kind of geometrical technique, I'm sure,

692
00:36:19,660 --> 00:36:21,660
with protractors and stuff.

693
00:36:21,660 --> 00:36:24,660
But the modern way of expressing it

694
00:36:24,660 --> 00:36:27,660
is this function,

695
00:36:27,660 --> 00:36:30,660
this projection function.

696
00:36:30,660 --> 00:36:32,660
And then we can make an assertion.

697
00:36:32,660 --> 00:36:34,660
And this assertion is where the power

698
00:36:34,660 --> 00:36:38,660
of the model comes from.

699
00:36:38,660 --> 00:36:40,660
I could take a different data set,

700
00:36:40,660 --> 00:36:44,660
and I could apply the same model,

701
00:36:44,660 --> 00:36:46,660
and I get a different map.

702
00:36:46,660 --> 00:36:49,660
This is Mercator's original map.

703
00:36:49,660 --> 00:36:51,660
This time, Europe is in the center.

704
00:36:51,660 --> 00:36:54,660
Specifically, Belgium is in the center

705
00:36:54,660 --> 00:36:57,660
because Mercator was from Belgium.

706
00:36:57,660 --> 00:37:01,660
But you can see that the map is essentially the same.

707
00:37:01,660 --> 00:37:03,660
A lot of details are different

708
00:37:03,660 --> 00:37:05,660
because their data set wasn't as good.

709
00:37:05,660 --> 00:37:08,660
But if you look at Europe and Mediterranean,

710
00:37:08,660 --> 00:37:10,660
the data is pretty good.

711
00:37:10,660 --> 00:37:13,660
You look over in North America,

712
00:37:13,660 --> 00:37:15,660
and the data's okay.

713
00:37:15,660 --> 00:37:17,660
South America is actually a little better

714
00:37:17,660 --> 00:37:19,660
on the East Coast.

715
00:37:19,660 --> 00:37:20,660
The West Coast of South America

716
00:37:20,660 --> 00:37:25,660
is weirdly sort of shifted to the East.

717
00:37:25,660 --> 00:37:28,660
I think there's an offset error in the data.

718
00:37:28,660 --> 00:37:31,660
And the West Coast of North America

719
00:37:31,660 --> 00:37:33,660
is kind of vague.

720
00:37:33,660 --> 00:37:35,660
It's like they knew there was a West Coast,

721
00:37:35,660 --> 00:37:37,660
so they just sketched it in.

722
00:37:37,660 --> 00:37:39,660
We could call that an extrapolation

723
00:37:39,660 --> 00:37:42,660
or an interpolation of incomplete data.

724
00:37:42,660 --> 00:37:45,660
And, boy, this starts to feel to me

725
00:37:45,660 --> 00:37:48,660
like the kind of things we do on software projects.

726
00:37:48,660 --> 00:37:49,660
We take a data set

727
00:37:49,660 --> 00:37:52,660
that is imperfect and incomplete.

728
00:37:52,660 --> 00:37:55,660
We apply transformations to that data

729
00:37:55,660 --> 00:38:01,660
that allow us to make meaningful decisions.

730
00:38:01,660 --> 00:38:03,660
The decision in this case is,

731
00:38:03,660 --> 00:38:07,660
which way should I point the ship?

732
00:38:07,660 --> 00:38:10,660
So the domain is just anything

733
00:38:10,660 --> 00:38:13,660
that you could know about.

734
00:38:13,660 --> 00:38:17,660
But the model is a system of abstractions

735
00:38:17,660 --> 00:38:19,660
representing some selection

736
00:38:19,660 --> 00:38:22,660
of the imperfect aspects of that domain.

737
00:38:22,660 --> 00:38:26,660
This is a very basic definition of model.

738
00:38:26,660 --> 00:38:30,660
But I think any time we start to get more concrete,

739
00:38:30,660 --> 00:38:32,660
more hands-on than that,

740
00:38:32,660 --> 00:38:35,660
we lose the power of the model.

741
00:38:35,660 --> 00:38:40,660
The power of the model is that it is a set of concepts.

742
00:38:40,660 --> 00:38:42,660
But it's not neutral.

743
00:38:42,660 --> 00:38:47,660
It distills some assumptions and knowledge that we have.

744
00:38:47,660 --> 00:38:50,660
And some models are good for some things.

745
00:38:50,660 --> 00:38:53,660
Other models are good for other things.

746
00:38:53,660 --> 00:38:57,660
This is not a way of representing reality.

747
00:38:57,660 --> 00:39:01,660
In fact, I think realism is a distraction.

748
00:39:01,660 --> 00:39:05,660
Consider, is this a realistic picture of the world?

749
00:39:05,660 --> 00:39:08,660
Well, you know, we discussed, for example,

750
00:39:08,660 --> 00:39:14,660
that Greenland is not the size of Africa.

751
00:39:14,660 --> 00:39:15,660
Maybe this, then,

752
00:39:15,660 --> 00:39:18,660
is a more realistic picture of the world.

753
00:39:18,660 --> 00:39:22,660
Greenland and Africa are in their proper perspectives.

754
00:39:22,660 --> 00:39:26,660
But somehow the idea that, you know,

755
00:39:26,660 --> 00:39:28,660
if you went east from Africa,

756
00:39:28,660 --> 00:39:30,660
you would just end up in space

757
00:39:30,660 --> 00:39:32,660
doesn't feel right either.

758
00:39:32,660 --> 00:39:35,660
So neither of these is realistic,

759
00:39:35,660 --> 00:39:38,660
nor are they trying to be.

760
00:39:38,660 --> 00:39:41,660
This is a pretty realistic picture of the world.

761
00:39:41,660 --> 00:39:44,660
If I were a navigator at sea, though,

762
00:39:44,660 --> 00:39:48,660
I would rather have either of those other two than this.

763
00:39:48,660 --> 00:39:50,660
And I would much prefer the Mercator map

764
00:39:50,660 --> 00:39:52,660
over the Dymaxion one,

765
00:39:52,660 --> 00:39:54,660
but I would settle for either

766
00:39:54,660 --> 00:39:56,660
rather than this very realistic picture.

767
00:39:56,660 --> 00:40:02,660
Realism doesn't give us models that we actually can use.

768
00:40:02,660 --> 00:40:07,660
Another thing about models is they need a narrow focus.

769
00:40:07,660 --> 00:40:12,660
I Googled for domain model and found this on the web.

770
00:40:12,660 --> 00:40:14,660
And I think there were some

771
00:40:14,660 --> 00:40:15,660
more complicated,

772
00:40:15,660 --> 00:40:18,660
but this was all I could really fit on a slide.

773
00:40:18,660 --> 00:40:21,660
Completeness really leads us off the track.

774
00:40:21,660 --> 00:40:22,660
And yet we say,

775
00:40:22,660 --> 00:40:25,660
but we have all this information.

776
00:40:25,660 --> 00:40:27,660
We have to represent it somehow.

777
00:40:27,660 --> 00:40:29,660
We have to get it into the computer.

778
00:40:29,660 --> 00:40:31,660
And yes, that's true.

779
00:40:31,660 --> 00:40:33,660
I could picture having a database

780
00:40:33,660 --> 00:40:36,660
where we just say this database is for archiving

781
00:40:36,660 --> 00:40:38,660
all the data we've got

782
00:40:38,660 --> 00:40:41,660
in some kind of canonical form.

783
00:40:41,660 --> 00:40:44,660
But that's not the kind of model I'm talking about.

784
00:40:44,660 --> 00:40:46,660
If you're going to do that,

785
00:40:46,660 --> 00:40:47,660
what you need to do then

786
00:40:47,660 --> 00:40:49,660
is have a separate representation

787
00:40:49,660 --> 00:40:52,660
of some small selection of that data

788
00:40:52,660 --> 00:40:55,660
that will be used to solve specific problems

789
00:40:55,660 --> 00:40:56,660
in the domain.

790
00:40:56,660 --> 00:40:59,660
And you'll transform the data back and forth.

791
00:40:59,660 --> 00:41:01,660
And I think that is, in fact,

792
00:41:01,660 --> 00:41:03,660
the recommended thing.

793
00:41:03,660 --> 00:41:05,660
That is, we want multiple models

794
00:41:05,660 --> 00:41:07,660
from multiple problems

795
00:41:07,660 --> 00:41:10,660
within a larger domain.

796
00:41:10,660 --> 00:41:14,660
So a model should be focused on

797
00:41:14,660 --> 00:41:17,660
a specific, difficult, important problem.

798
00:41:17,660 --> 00:41:21,660
This is obviously too hard to do for everything.

799
00:41:21,660 --> 00:41:23,660
Don't do this for everything.

800
00:41:23,660 --> 00:41:25,660
Do it for the hard problems,

801
00:41:25,660 --> 00:41:27,660
the ones that you don't think you'd get right

802
00:41:27,660 --> 00:41:29,660
if you just started writing,

803
00:41:29,660 --> 00:41:33,660
well, if this, then this.

804
00:41:33,660 --> 00:41:36,660
If you think you could get it right doing that,

805
00:41:36,660 --> 00:41:37,660
then do that.

806
00:41:37,660 --> 00:41:39,660
But if you don't think you could get it right doing that,

807
00:41:39,660 --> 00:41:43,660
maybe a model would be helpful.

808
00:41:43,660 --> 00:41:45,660
And for that, then you need to say,

809
00:41:45,660 --> 00:41:48,660
well, then this model would be helpful for what?

810
00:41:48,660 --> 00:41:50,660
For a specific use, right?

811
00:41:50,660 --> 00:41:52,660
A specific kind of problem.

812
00:41:52,660 --> 00:41:54,660
This is one of the reasons

813
00:41:54,660 --> 00:41:59,660
that I emphasize concrete examples.

814
00:41:59,660 --> 00:42:00,660
Earlier I asked,

815
00:42:00,660 --> 00:42:04,660
which of these two models do you like better?

816
00:42:04,660 --> 00:42:07,660
That's not really the right answer, the question.

817
00:42:07,660 --> 00:42:09,660
The question I should ask is,

818
00:42:09,660 --> 00:42:11,660
which do you think would be more useful?

819
00:42:11,660 --> 00:42:14,660
And when I ask the question like that,

820
00:42:14,660 --> 00:42:17,660
it might cause you to change the way you're thinking

821
00:42:17,660 --> 00:42:20,660
and think, well, useful for what?

822
00:42:20,660 --> 00:42:24,660
What am I being asked to do at using this model?

823
00:42:24,660 --> 00:42:28,660
And then you might say, well, okay,

824
00:42:28,660 --> 00:42:30,660
we need some scenarios,

825
00:42:30,660 --> 00:42:33,660
some specific stories about the business

826
00:42:33,660 --> 00:42:36,660
that tell us what we're going to use this thing for.

827
00:42:36,660 --> 00:42:38,660
And I did tell you one.

828
00:42:38,660 --> 00:42:40,660
I told you the initial booking.

829
00:42:40,660 --> 00:42:42,660
And you might say, well, for that purpose,

830
00:42:42,660 --> 00:42:44,660
I think either of these would be fine.

831
00:42:44,660 --> 00:42:47,660
I could describe the result of the initial booking

832
00:42:47,660 --> 00:42:49,660
using the series of stops,

833
00:42:49,660 --> 00:42:51,660
an itinerary as a series of stops

834
00:42:51,660 --> 00:42:53,660
or an itinerary as a series of legs.

835
00:42:53,660 --> 00:42:57,660
I don't think there would really be much difference.

836
00:42:57,660 --> 00:43:00,660
So let me give you another scenario,

837
00:43:00,660 --> 00:43:02,660
another thing we have to solve.

838
00:43:02,660 --> 00:43:06,660
And I think we can use the same model for both of these.

839
00:43:06,660 --> 00:43:08,660
Sometimes,

840
00:43:08,660 --> 00:43:10,660
the customer will call up and say something like,

841
00:43:10,660 --> 00:43:13,660
I want to send my cargo to Dallas.

842
00:43:13,660 --> 00:43:15,660
Then a little bit later,

843
00:43:15,660 --> 00:43:17,660
they will say, oh,

844
00:43:17,660 --> 00:43:20,660
I want to send my cargo to Seattle,

845
00:43:20,660 --> 00:43:21,660
not to Dallas.

846
00:43:21,660 --> 00:43:23,660
But by this time,

847
00:43:23,660 --> 00:43:25,660
the ship has already sailed.

848
00:43:25,660 --> 00:43:28,660
The cargo is somewhere in the middle of the Pacific Ocean.

849
00:43:28,660 --> 00:43:31,660
If only they had called a few days sooner,

850
00:43:31,660 --> 00:43:34,660
we could have just done the whole thing over again, right?

851
00:43:34,660 --> 00:43:37,660
Then it's simple from a software point of view.

852
00:43:37,660 --> 00:43:42,660
Because we can just undo everything we did

853
00:43:42,660 --> 00:43:44,660
and do it all from the beginning.

854
00:43:44,660 --> 00:43:47,660
But now we cannot do that.

855
00:43:47,660 --> 00:43:52,660
We need to do something to get rid of the legs.

856
00:43:52,660 --> 00:43:53,660
I mean, basically,

857
00:43:53,660 --> 00:43:57,660
this ship is going to go to Long Beach, California.

858
00:43:57,660 --> 00:44:00,660
And then is our next chance to change anything.

859
00:44:00,660 --> 00:44:02,660
Our cargo will be unloaded in Long Beach

860
00:44:02,660 --> 00:44:04,660
and we could put it on something else.

861
00:44:04,660 --> 00:44:06,660
We don't want to put it on to the leg

862
00:44:06,660 --> 00:44:08,660
that goes to Dallas.

863
00:44:08,660 --> 00:44:10,660
So we want to cancel that leg

864
00:44:10,660 --> 00:44:13,660
and replace it with something else.

865
00:44:13,660 --> 00:44:17,660
Now, the way that I am trying to explain the problem

866
00:44:17,660 --> 00:44:21,660
might lead us to think

867
00:44:21,660 --> 00:44:25,660
that the leg model might be helpful for this problem.

868
00:44:25,660 --> 00:44:27,660
Because it's so natural

869
00:44:27,660 --> 00:44:30,660
to talk about canceling a leg from Long Beach to Dallas

870
00:44:30,660 --> 00:44:34,660
and replacing it with a leg from Long Beach to Seattle.

871
00:44:34,660 --> 00:44:35,660
But I can do this.

872
00:44:35,660 --> 00:44:37,660
But I can do it the other way.

873
00:44:37,660 --> 00:44:38,660
And I will try.

874
00:44:38,660 --> 00:44:42,660
Because it's worth looking at our alternatives.

875
00:44:42,660 --> 00:44:45,660
We could cancel the unloading portion of the stop in Long Beach

876
00:44:45,660 --> 00:44:46,660
and the stop in Dallas.

877
00:44:46,660 --> 00:44:48,660
Merge the remaining part of the Long Beach stop

878
00:44:48,660 --> 00:44:49,660
with the load portion of the stop

879
00:44:49,660 --> 00:44:51,660
that sets the cargo bound for Seattle

880
00:44:51,660 --> 00:44:54,660
and add a stop at the end that unloads in Seattle.

881
00:44:54,660 --> 00:44:56,660
That's not so bad.

882
00:45:00,660 --> 00:45:03,660
Here's a pseudocode version of that.

883
00:45:03,660 --> 00:45:05,660
And it is actually something

884
00:45:05,660 --> 00:45:06,660
you could do.

885
00:45:06,660 --> 00:45:09,660
This is not out of the realm of reasonable solutions.

886
00:45:09,660 --> 00:45:13,660
But here is the solution with the leg.

887
00:45:13,660 --> 00:45:15,660
And not only is it fewer steps,

888
00:45:15,660 --> 00:45:17,660
but each one is less confusing.

889
00:45:17,660 --> 00:45:20,660
It's not so much the number of steps here.

890
00:45:20,660 --> 00:45:22,660
It's how tricky some of those steps are.

891
00:45:22,660 --> 00:45:25,660
The ones that involve taking a stop apart,

892
00:45:25,660 --> 00:45:27,660
putting it back together again.

893
00:45:27,660 --> 00:45:29,660
In the leg-based one,

894
00:45:29,660 --> 00:45:32,660
we take things apart between, you know, we...

895
00:45:32,660 --> 00:45:34,660
So...

896
00:45:35,660 --> 00:45:38,660
Back to our original design.

897
00:45:38,660 --> 00:45:41,660
If we're going to transform this thing,

898
00:45:41,660 --> 00:45:43,660
it did look like this.

899
00:45:43,660 --> 00:45:45,660
And now it's going to look something like this.

900
00:45:45,660 --> 00:45:47,660
When you...

901
00:45:47,660 --> 00:45:50,660
You're still going to send the origin and destination

902
00:45:50,660 --> 00:45:52,660
to the routing service,

903
00:45:52,660 --> 00:45:54,660
but instead of updating the database,

904
00:45:54,660 --> 00:45:57,660
it's going to create an itinerary.

905
00:45:57,660 --> 00:45:59,660
Pass that back.

906
00:45:59,660 --> 00:46:03,660
Notice that we now have rid ourselves

907
00:46:03,660 --> 00:46:04,660
of that side effect.

908
00:46:04,660 --> 00:46:07,660
The routing service no longer needs

909
00:46:07,660 --> 00:46:09,660
to update the database.

910
00:46:09,660 --> 00:46:11,660
I mean, someone could make it

911
00:46:11,660 --> 00:46:13,660
so that it updates the database,

912
00:46:13,660 --> 00:46:15,660
and I imagine they will

913
00:46:15,660 --> 00:46:17,660
if we don't communicate very well.

914
00:46:17,660 --> 00:46:19,660
But this is a great opportunity

915
00:46:19,660 --> 00:46:21,660
to improve the basic properties,

916
00:46:21,660 --> 00:46:23,660
the sort of architectural properties

917
00:46:23,660 --> 00:46:25,660
of the design.

918
00:46:25,660 --> 00:46:27,660
And it comes from having a very explicit way

919
00:46:27,660 --> 00:46:30,660
to talk about the results of this computation.

920
00:46:32,660 --> 00:46:34,660
Another thing we talk about

921
00:46:34,660 --> 00:46:38,660
earlier is the disconnected itinerary, remember?

922
00:46:38,660 --> 00:46:40,660
Now, if we...

923
00:46:40,660 --> 00:46:43,660
Previously, we were putting integrity constraints

924
00:46:43,660 --> 00:46:45,660
in the database and stuff like that.

925
00:46:45,660 --> 00:46:48,660
But now that we have a little more way

926
00:46:48,660 --> 00:46:54,660
of describing the result of routing,

927
00:46:54,660 --> 00:46:57,660
we could talk about how this could be done

928
00:46:57,660 --> 00:46:59,660
in terms of this model.

929
00:46:59,660 --> 00:47:01,660
Does this model lend itself to telling you

930
00:47:01,660 --> 00:47:04,660
whether an itinerary is disconnected?

931
00:47:04,660 --> 00:47:07,660
I think there's a fairly straightforward way

932
00:47:07,660 --> 00:47:09,660
of doing it.

933
00:47:09,660 --> 00:47:13,660
So you could say that an itinerary is connected

934
00:47:13,660 --> 00:47:16,660
when the end location of each leg

935
00:47:16,660 --> 00:47:18,660
is the same as the start location

936
00:47:18,660 --> 00:47:19,660
of the next leg.

937
00:47:19,660 --> 00:47:21,660
That's very natural.

938
00:47:21,660 --> 00:47:23,660
You could talk about that with a business person

939
00:47:23,660 --> 00:47:25,660
and find out, perhaps,

940
00:47:25,660 --> 00:47:27,660
that that's not really what they mean,

941
00:47:27,660 --> 00:47:30,660
or find out that, yes, you've got it right.

942
00:47:30,660 --> 00:47:32,660
And that looks almost like...

943
00:47:32,660 --> 00:47:36,660
And that looks almost like pseudocode, right?

944
00:47:36,660 --> 00:47:39,660
You could say, yeah, I could code that.

945
00:47:39,660 --> 00:47:41,660
Now, it looks so much like pseudocode

946
00:47:41,660 --> 00:47:43,660
that I want to point out something.

947
00:47:43,660 --> 00:47:46,660
I don't mean when I say an itinerary is connected when.

948
00:47:46,660 --> 00:47:49,660
I don't mean I'm describing the calculation

949
00:47:49,660 --> 00:47:51,660
that you should do.

950
00:47:51,660 --> 00:47:53,660
What I'm really hoping to do

951
00:47:53,660 --> 00:47:55,660
is define a new word

952
00:47:55,660 --> 00:47:57,660
that will be part of our language

953
00:47:57,660 --> 00:47:59,660
and a new concept.

954
00:47:59,660 --> 00:48:01,660
In other words, I'm saying that the model

955
00:48:01,660 --> 00:48:03,660
is not just those three objects,

956
00:48:03,660 --> 00:48:06,660
a cargo, an itinerary, and some legs.

957
00:48:06,660 --> 00:48:08,660
It's not just those objects.

958
00:48:08,660 --> 00:48:10,660
It's also...

959
00:48:10,660 --> 00:48:12,660
It describes the way those objects

960
00:48:12,660 --> 00:48:14,660
are related to each other.

961
00:48:14,660 --> 00:48:17,660
So, for example, one of the properties

962
00:48:17,660 --> 00:48:19,660
that an itinerary might have is,

963
00:48:19,660 --> 00:48:21,660
is it connected?

964
00:48:21,660 --> 00:48:24,660
And that's part of the concept of an itinerary.

965
00:48:24,660 --> 00:48:26,660
It's not just a calculation that we can do,

966
00:48:26,660 --> 00:48:28,660
but it does correspond

967
00:48:28,660 --> 00:48:30,660
to a calculation we can do.

968
00:48:30,660 --> 00:48:32,660
Being able to...

969
00:48:32,660 --> 00:48:34,660
But being able to step back from the calculation

970
00:48:34,660 --> 00:48:36,660
and say this is actually a concept

971
00:48:36,660 --> 00:48:38,660
in that sense that I talked about

972
00:48:38,660 --> 00:48:40,660
what is the model earlier,

973
00:48:40,660 --> 00:48:42,660
that's a very key thing.

974
00:48:42,660 --> 00:48:45,660
We're not just rearranging

975
00:48:45,660 --> 00:48:47,660
the code in some way.

976
00:48:49,660 --> 00:48:51,660
When we get to this point

977
00:48:51,660 --> 00:48:53,660
where we can talk about

978
00:48:53,660 --> 00:48:55,660
the solution that we have,

979
00:48:55,660 --> 00:48:57,660
talk about our software,

980
00:48:57,660 --> 00:48:59,660
the same way with another software

981
00:48:59,660 --> 00:49:03,660
person as we can with a business person,

982
00:49:03,660 --> 00:49:05,660
and where we can have real discussions

983
00:49:05,660 --> 00:49:07,660
with them about the things

984
00:49:07,660 --> 00:49:09,660
that need to be done

985
00:49:09,660 --> 00:49:12,660
and how we might do them differently.

986
00:49:12,660 --> 00:49:14,660
We call that the ubiquitous language.

987
00:49:14,660 --> 00:49:16,660
The ubiquitous language is one of those

988
00:49:16,660 --> 00:49:19,660
fundamentals of domain-driven design.

989
00:49:19,660 --> 00:49:22,660
You can see throughout the whole example

990
00:49:22,660 --> 00:49:24,660
that I've emphasized language,

991
00:49:24,660 --> 00:49:26,660
looking at how we talk about the problem

992
00:49:26,660 --> 00:49:29,660
as being fundamental to finding a good solution.

993
00:49:29,660 --> 00:49:32,660
So ubiquitous language is one of those

994
00:49:32,660 --> 00:49:34,660
three or four concepts.

995
00:49:34,660 --> 00:49:37,660
One thing about that word,

996
00:49:37,660 --> 00:49:39,660
that phrase ubiquitous language,

997
00:49:39,660 --> 00:49:43,660
is that it kind of conveys

998
00:49:43,660 --> 00:49:48,660
a ubiquitousness in all sense.

999
00:49:48,660 --> 00:49:50,660
That your whole system would revolve

1000
00:49:50,660 --> 00:49:52,660
around one language.

1001
00:49:52,660 --> 00:49:55,660
And that is emphatically not what we mean.

1002
00:49:55,660 --> 00:49:59,660
So I like to pin ubiquitous language

1003
00:49:59,660 --> 00:50:02,660
as this within a bounded context.

1004
00:50:02,660 --> 00:50:04,660
I'm not going to talk about bounded context

1005
00:50:04,660 --> 00:50:07,660
right now, but Cyril will in a couple of hours.

1006
00:50:07,660 --> 00:50:10,660
I can't remember exactly when he was in the agenda.

1007
00:50:10,660 --> 00:50:13,660
So we'll go into depth on that concept.

1008
00:50:13,660 --> 00:50:15,660
But the idea is basically that we will have

1009
00:50:15,660 --> 00:50:19,660
multiple models with distinct languages

1010
00:50:19,660 --> 00:50:22,660
and that that whole assembly of models

1011
00:50:22,660 --> 00:50:24,660
will provide us with the solution

1012
00:50:24,660 --> 00:50:27,660
to the various problems that we have.

1013
00:50:29,660 --> 00:50:33,660
So getting back to the question of why do this stuff,

1014
00:50:33,660 --> 00:50:36,660
I think that the answer lies in complexity

1015
00:50:36,660 --> 00:50:38,660
within the domain itself.

1016
00:50:38,660 --> 00:50:42,660
Sometimes we have complexity

1017
00:50:42,660 --> 00:50:45,660
in the technical solutions that we need to do.

1018
00:50:45,660 --> 00:50:49,660
If we were, perhaps if we were, say,

1019
00:50:49,660 --> 00:50:52,660
in the early days of creating Twitter.

1020
00:50:52,660 --> 00:50:56,660
Twitter is pretty simple in its logic,

1021
00:50:56,660 --> 00:50:58,660
but especially early Twitter was.

1022
00:50:58,660 --> 00:51:02,660
But at the time, the idea that you would

1023
00:51:02,660 --> 00:51:06,660
send out millions of messages in a timely way

1024
00:51:06,660 --> 00:51:09,660
to someone's, you know, to the followers,

1025
00:51:09,660 --> 00:51:12,660
all those things, that was a real challenge at that time.

1026
00:51:12,660 --> 00:51:15,660
Now because of the frameworks that they created

1027
00:51:15,660 --> 00:51:19,660
and made public, it's not actually that hard to do now.

1028
00:51:19,660 --> 00:51:21,660
If I wanted to go create something

1029
00:51:21,660 --> 00:51:23,660
on the scale of Twitter, I think I could do it

1030
00:51:23,660 --> 00:51:25,660
all by myself.

1031
00:51:25,660 --> 00:51:28,660
That probably couldn't get very many people

1032
00:51:28,660 --> 00:51:30,660
to sign up, though.

1033
00:51:30,660 --> 00:51:32,660
But the point being, then,

1034
00:51:32,660 --> 00:51:36,660
that the Twitter of that time

1035
00:51:36,660 --> 00:51:39,660
would not have met this criteria, right?

1036
00:51:39,660 --> 00:51:42,660
The critical complexity that they were dealing with

1037
00:51:42,660 --> 00:51:45,660
was a technical scaling complexity.

1038
00:51:45,660 --> 00:51:47,660
Whereas most of the time on the projects

1039
00:51:47,660 --> 00:51:49,660
that I've been working on,

1040
00:51:49,660 --> 00:51:51,660
the critical complexity is in the domain itself.

1041
00:51:51,660 --> 00:51:54,660
It is that shipping is very complex.

1042
00:51:54,660 --> 00:51:57,660
And if we do our job right,

1043
00:51:57,660 --> 00:51:59,660
our software might not actually be

1044
00:51:59,660 --> 00:52:02,660
more complex than shipping itself.

1045
00:52:02,660 --> 00:52:04,660
But if we are not careful,

1046
00:52:04,660 --> 00:52:07,660
our software might be much more complex

1047
00:52:07,660 --> 00:52:09,660
than the actual shipping domain.

1048
00:52:09,660 --> 00:52:12,660
And because the actual shipping domain

1049
00:52:12,660 --> 00:52:15,660
is probably pretty close to the minimum complexity

1050
00:52:15,660 --> 00:52:18,660
that we can hope for, right?

1051
00:52:18,660 --> 00:52:23,660
Well, that is basically my time.

1052
00:52:23,660 --> 00:52:25,660
But I want to just touch on one other thing.

1053
00:52:25,660 --> 00:52:27,660
Suppose that in some way,

1054
00:52:27,660 --> 00:52:34,660
instead of presenting the scenario that I did

1055
00:52:34,660 --> 00:52:37,660
as the second scenario where in mid-transit

1056
00:52:37,660 --> 00:52:39,660
they changed the destination.

1057
00:52:39,660 --> 00:52:41,660
Suppose instead I said,

1058
00:52:41,660 --> 00:52:44,660
well, we're still on our original itinerary,

1059
00:52:44,660 --> 00:52:46,660
but we're about to arrive at the first port

1060
00:52:46,660 --> 00:52:48,660
and we need to generate these purchase orders

1061
00:52:48,660 --> 00:52:51,660
to tell the people in the ports

1062
00:52:51,660 --> 00:52:53,660
how to actually unload the cargoes,

1063
00:52:53,660 --> 00:52:55,660
what to do with each cargo.

1064
00:52:55,660 --> 00:52:57,660
And there are these companies

1065
00:52:57,660 --> 00:52:58,660
that operate in ports,

1066
00:52:58,660 --> 00:52:59,660
and we need to figure out

1067
00:52:59,660 --> 00:53:01,660
which one to send each instruction to,

1068
00:53:01,660 --> 00:53:03,660
and we need to get the whole story

1069
00:53:03,660 --> 00:53:05,660
of each of thousands of cargoes right,

1070
00:53:05,660 --> 00:53:11,660
criss-crossing through an enormous, complex port.

1071
00:53:11,660 --> 00:53:13,660
And when you look at this problem,

1072
00:53:13,660 --> 00:53:14,660
you start to think,

1073
00:53:14,660 --> 00:53:17,660
wow, the things that happen in the port

1074
00:53:17,660 --> 00:53:20,660
are more complicated than the things happening

1075
00:53:20,660 --> 00:53:23,660
in the routing of the ship.

1076
00:53:23,660 --> 00:53:26,660
Maybe my model should be re-geared around that.

1077
00:53:26,660 --> 00:53:30,660
And that might point me to the stops model.

1078
00:53:30,660 --> 00:53:32,660
Because stop has a very explicit place

1079
00:53:32,660 --> 00:53:34,660
to talk about the port.

1080
00:53:34,660 --> 00:53:37,660
The stop represents everything that happens in a port.

1081
00:53:37,660 --> 00:53:40,660
The leg says everything that happens between the ports.

1082
00:53:40,660 --> 00:53:43,660
It literally has no place to put information

1083
00:53:43,660 --> 00:53:46,660
about what's happening in the port.

1084
00:53:46,660 --> 00:53:48,660
And so you would have ended up

1085
00:53:48,660 --> 00:53:50,660
with a quite different model.

1086
00:53:50,660 --> 00:53:53,660
It isn't like we're converging to the best model.

1087
00:53:53,660 --> 00:53:55,660
We are converging to a model

1088
00:53:55,660 --> 00:53:59,660
that's very path-dependent,

1089
00:53:59,660 --> 00:54:01,660
depending on the particular problems

1090
00:54:01,660 --> 00:54:03,660
that we are trying to solve

1091
00:54:03,660 --> 00:54:05,660
and the order in which we try to solve them

1092
00:54:05,660 --> 00:54:07,660
and the ideas that happen to come up

1093
00:54:07,660 --> 00:54:09,660
during that process.

1094
00:54:09,660 --> 00:54:13,660
So if you encounter a different sort of problem

1095
00:54:13,660 --> 00:54:15,660
later on,

1096
00:54:15,660 --> 00:54:17,660
you have some choices.

1097
00:54:17,660 --> 00:54:19,660
You could say, well, let's bite the bullet

1098
00:54:19,660 --> 00:54:21,660
and change to stops now.

1099
00:54:21,660 --> 00:54:23,660
And you're accepting that your solution

1100
00:54:23,660 --> 00:54:25,660
to the routing problem will be

1101
00:54:25,660 --> 00:54:27,660
slightly more complex.

1102
00:54:27,660 --> 00:54:28,660
Remember I said, though,

1103
00:54:28,660 --> 00:54:30,660
it's not out of the question, right?

1104
00:54:30,660 --> 00:54:32,660
It's doable.

1105
00:54:32,660 --> 00:54:35,660
But we'll reserve our best choices

1106
00:54:35,660 --> 00:54:37,660
for the most difficult problem,

1107
00:54:37,660 --> 00:54:39,660
which is the one in port.

1108
00:54:39,660 --> 00:54:42,660
Or maybe this is time for two bounded contexts.

1109
00:54:42,660 --> 00:54:44,660
I'll solve route-related problems

1110
00:54:44,660 --> 00:54:46,660
over here with legs.

1111
00:54:46,660 --> 00:54:48,660
We'll have another place where we can solve

1112
00:54:48,660 --> 00:54:50,660
problems in ports using stops.

1113
00:54:50,660 --> 00:54:52,660
And I'll have to transform the data

1114
00:54:52,660 --> 00:54:54,660
back and forth, back and forth.

1115
00:54:54,660 --> 00:54:56,660
But that's doable, too.

1116
00:54:56,660 --> 00:54:58,660
Or a third choice,

1117
00:54:58,660 --> 00:55:00,660
which is stick with legs and go ahead

1118
00:55:00,660 --> 00:55:03,660
and cram the new functionality on there somehow.

1119
00:55:03,660 --> 00:55:06,660
You know, I can deal with the first vendor

1120
00:55:06,660 --> 00:55:09,660
on the unload of the incoming leg

1121
00:55:09,660 --> 00:55:11,660
and the last vendor on the outgoing leg.

1122
00:55:11,660 --> 00:55:13,660
Then I'll just put all the other vendors

1123
00:55:13,660 --> 00:55:15,660
onto the incoming leg because

1124
00:55:15,660 --> 00:55:17,660
I've got to go somewhere.

1125
00:55:17,660 --> 00:55:21,660
And that is also sometimes a valid choice,

1126
00:55:21,660 --> 00:55:23,660
but especially worth mentioning

1127
00:55:23,660 --> 00:55:25,660
because it is the default choice.

1128
00:55:25,660 --> 00:55:28,660
That's the choice you make if you don't recognize

1129
00:55:28,660 --> 00:55:30,660
that there's a choice to make.

1130
00:55:30,660 --> 00:55:33,660
And that's a choice that gets made all the time.

1131
00:55:33,660 --> 00:55:35,660
In fact, I think probably on most projects,

1132
00:55:35,660 --> 00:55:37,660
it's sort of a time thing.

1133
00:55:37,660 --> 00:55:40,660
How long is it before you make that choice?

1134
00:55:40,660 --> 00:55:42,660
And once you've made that choice,

1135
00:55:42,660 --> 00:55:45,660
you're kind of stuck because you have a model

1136
00:55:45,660 --> 00:55:48,660
that no longer fits the most difficult problems

1137
00:55:48,660 --> 00:55:50,660
you're trying to deal with.

1138
00:55:50,660 --> 00:55:52,660
So you're not getting much benefit from modeling.

1139
00:55:52,660 --> 00:55:54,660
And that's the last thing I'll say

1140
00:55:54,660 --> 00:55:56,660
before I turn it over to the next person.

1141
00:55:56,660 --> 00:55:58,660
Remember that having a model

1142
00:55:58,660 --> 00:56:02,660
doesn't actually give you any advantage as such.

1143
00:56:02,660 --> 00:56:05,660
The advantage doesn't come from having a model.

1144
00:56:05,660 --> 00:56:08,660
The advantage comes from having a model

1145
00:56:08,660 --> 00:56:11,660
that is a very nice fit to the specific set of problems

1146
00:56:11,660 --> 00:56:15,660
that you are trying to solve in that context.

1147
00:56:15,660 --> 00:56:18,660
If you have a model, no matter how nice

1148
00:56:18,660 --> 00:56:22,660
its properties might be in an abstract sense,

1149
00:56:22,660 --> 00:56:24,660
but it doesn't fit the particular problems

1150
00:56:24,660 --> 00:56:26,660
that you are trying to solve,

1151
00:56:26,660 --> 00:56:28,660
it will not give you any benefit.

1152
00:56:28,660 --> 00:56:31,660
It might be fighting against you.

1153
00:56:31,660 --> 00:56:35,660
So I'll go out on that point.

1154
00:56:35,660 --> 00:56:38,660
And I hope that this example helped to illustrate

1155
00:56:38,660 --> 00:56:40,660
the spirit of domain-driven design.

1156
00:56:40,660 --> 00:56:42,660
And I'll leave it to the other guys

1157
00:56:42,660 --> 00:56:49,660
to go into the various more detailed parts of it.

1158
00:56:49,660 --> 00:56:50,660
Thank you.

1159
00:56:52,660 --> 00:56:53,660
Thank you.
